CBP MODEL FOR WELLMED POPULATION

The CBP measure is defined as the percentage of members aged 18–85 who have been diagnosed with hypertension (HTN) and have adequately controlled blood pressure (BP) during the measurement year.  

Adequate control is specified as having a representative systolic BP of less than 140 mm Hg and a representative diastolic BP of less than 90 mm Hg.  

A representative BP reading is the most recent BP reading taken during the measurement year, on or after the second hypertension diagnosis. If multiple BP readings are taken on the same date or recorded in the chart on the same date, the lowest systolic and diastolic BP readings are used.  

If no BP is recorded during the measurement year, the member is assumed to be “not controlled.” 

 

Model Characteristics  

Machine Learning Classification Model - XGBoost.  

6, 9 &12 months rolling look back period is used for model features. 

Features related to PDC (Proportion of Days Covered) are also used. 

1900 features tested – Only 214 distinct features chosen for the final monthly models. 

Compliant vs Non-Compliant class balance: 75% vs 25%.

 

 

Main categories of the features used are summarized in the table below: 

 

